= entry .Uuid   a 128-bit universally unique identifier, as its two 64-bit halves
-   detail
+   detail

-       A `Uuid` is a case class of its most and least significant 64 bits, `#msb` and
-       `#lsb`. It is made fresh with `Uuid()`, which draws a random (version 4) identifier
-       from `java.util.UUID.randomUUID`; written literally with `uuid"…"`; parsed from text
-       with `.parse`, which raises `Uuid.Error`, or with `.extract`, which gives an
-       `Optional`; or built directly from its two halves.
+       Un `Uuid` es una case class de sus 64 bits más y menos significativos, `#msb` y `#lsb`.
+       Se crea nuevo con `Uuid()`, que extrae un identificador aleatorio (versión 4) de
+       `java.util.UUID.randomUUID`; se escribe literalmente con `uuid"…"`; se analiza a partir de
+       texto con `.parse`, que lanza `Uuid.Error`, o con `.extract`, que da un `Optional`; o se
+       construye directamente a partir de sus dos mitades.

-       The companion is also an extractor, so a pattern matches text which is a valid UUID:
+       El objeto compañero es también un extractor, así que un patrón coincide con un texto que
+       sea un UUID válido:

=       The companion is also an extractor, so a pattern matches text which is a valid UUID:
=
-       ```scala
+       ```scala

=       The companion is also an extractor, so a pattern matches text which is a valid UUID:
=
=       ```scala
-       import soundness.*
+       import soundness.*

-       t"not-a-uuid" match
+       t"not-a-uuid" match

-         case Uuid(id) => id
+         case Uuid(id) => id

-         case _        => Uuid()
+         case _        => Uuid()

=         case _        => Uuid()
-       ```
+       ```

-       A `Uuid` renders as its canonical lowercase hyphenated form through `#text`, `show`,
-       `encode`, `inspect` and as a message; `uuid.in[Data]` gives its sixteen bytes, most
-       significant half first, each half big-endian. `#^` and `#unary_~` treat it as the bit
-       pattern it is, for deriving one identifier from others.
+       Un `Uuid` se representa en su forma canónica en minúsculas con guiones mediante `#text`,
+       `show`, `encode`, `inspect` y como mensaje; `uuid.in[Data]` da sus dieciséis bytes, con
+       la mitad más significativa primero y cada mitad en big-endian. `#^` y `#unary_~` lo
+       tratan como el patrón de bits que es, para derivar un identificador a partir de otros.

